d/Nien_K%C3%AAng-yao\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Nien K\u00eang-yao\">Nien K\u00eang-yao</a></div><div class=\"wst-header-forward-arrow\">\u2192</div></div></div><div class=\"wst-header-notes header&#95;notes searchaux wst-unknown-notes\"><style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r14791120\">.mw-parser-output .plainSister{display:block;font-size:93%;line-height:normal;list-style-type:none;list-style-image:none;list-style-position:outside;border:1px solid var(--border-color-interactive--hover,#AAA);float:right;clear:right;margin:0.5ex 0 0.5ex 0.5ex;padding:0;background:var(--background-color-base,#FFFFFF);color:var(--color-base,#202122)}.mw-parser-output .sisicon,.mw-parser-output .dabicon{padding-right:1ex}</style><ul class=\"plainSister\"><li class=\"sisitem\"><span class=\"sisicon\"><span typeof=\"mw:File/Frameless\"><a href=\"/wiki/Special:SiteMatrix\" title=\"Special:SiteMatrix\"><img alt=\"Sister Projects\" src=\"//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Wikimedia-logo.svg/20px-Wikimedia-logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikisource.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"18\" height=\"18\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Wikimedia-logo.svg/40px-Wikimedia-logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikisource.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x\" data-file-width=\"1024\" data-file-height=\"1024\" /></a></span></span><a href=\"/wiki/Special:SiteMatrix\" title=\"Special:SiteMatrix\">sister projects</a>: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ni_Yuanlu\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"w:Ni Yuanlu\">Wikipedia article</a>, <a href=\"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q107367609\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"d:Q107367609\">Wikidata item</a></li></ul></div><div class=\"ws-noexport\" id=\"ws-data\" style=\"speak:none\"><span id=\"ws-article-id\">3649143</span><span id=\"ws-title\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period\">Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period</a>, Volume 1 \u2014 Ni Y\u00fcan-lu</span><span id=\"ws-editor\">Arthur W. Hummel</span><span id=\"ws-author\">George A. Kennedy</span></div></div>\n<p><span style=\"display:none\" id=\"dynamic&#95;layout&#95;overrider\">Layout 2</span>\n</p>\n<div class=\"prp-pages-output\" lang=\"en\">\n<p><span><span class=\"pagenum ws-pagenum\" id=\"587\" data-page-number=\"587\" data-page-name=\"Page:Eminent Chinese Of The Ch\u2019ing Period - Hummel - 1943 - Vol. 1.pdf/599\" data-page-index=\"599\" data-page-quality=\"3\" title=\"Page:Eminent&#95;Chinese&#95;Of&#95;The&#95;Ch%E2%80%99ing&#95;Period&#95;-&#95;Hummel&#95;-&#95;1943&#95;-&#95;Vol.&#95;1.pdf/599\"><span id=\"pageindex&#95;599\" class=\"pagenum-inner ws-noexport\">&#8203;</span></span></span><b>NI</b> Y\u00fcan-lu \u502a\u5143\u7490 (<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r14767791\">.mw-parser-output .wst-tooltip{cursor:help;border-bottom:thin dotted cornflowerblue}.mw-parser-output .wst-tooltip-nodash{border-bottom:none}</style><span class=\"wst-tooltip wst-tooltip-dash\" title=\"Courtesy name (Tz\u00fb/Zi, \u5b57)\">T.</span>&#160;\u7389\u6c5d,&#32;<link rel=\"mw-deduplicated-inline-style\" href=\"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r14767791\" /><span class=\"wst-tooltip wst-tooltip-dash\" title=\"Literary name (Hao, \u865f)\">H.</span>&#160;\u9d3b\u5bf6), Jan. 7, 1594\u20131644, Apr. 25, Ming official, was a native of Shang-y\u00fc, Chekiang. Becoming a <i>chin-shih</i> in 1622, he entered the Hanlin Academy as a bachelor. While supervising examinations in Kiangsi in 1627 he offended the party of the eunuch, <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Wei_Chung-hsien\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Wei Chung-hsien\">Wei Chung-hsien</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>], but was saved from punishment by the latter's downfall at the close of that year. He continued his outspoken opposition to former members of that party and was one of the first to defend the Tung-lin society. It was at his request that the engraved blocks of the <i>San-ch'ao yao-tien</i>\u2014a work which had been compiled to discredit the Tung-lin group\u2014were destroyed (see under <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/F%C3%AAng_Ch%27%C3%BCan\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/F\u00eang Ch&#39;\u00fcan\">F\u00eang Ch'\u00fcan</a></span>). In 1635 he was promoted to the rank of libationer in the Academy, but shortly thereafter was forced into retirement by his enemies, remaining in seclusion until 1642 when he was made junior vice-president of the Board of War and lecturer to the Emperor. In the following year he was transferred to the presidency of the Board of Revenue where he attempted to correct abuses that had arisen in the system of taxation. <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Li_Tz%C5%AD-ch%27%C3%AAng\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Li Tz\u016d-ch&#39;\u00eang\">Li Tz\u016d-ch'\u00eang</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>] took the capital on April 25, 1644. Rather than fall into enemy hands Ni committed suicide on that day. He was given the posthumous name W\u00ean-ch\u00eang \u6587\u6b63 both by the Ming and Ch'ing regimes.\n</p><p>A collection of his literary works, entitled <i>Ni W\u00ean-ch\u00eang chi</i> (\u96c6), is preserved in the '<i>Ch'ien-k'un ch\u00eang-ch'i chi</i> (see under <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Huang_Tao-chou\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Huang Tao-chou\">Huang Tao-chou</a></span>). A short treatise of his on taxation, and a commentary to the <i>Classic of Changes</i> can be found in the collectanea <i>Hs\u00fceh-hai lei-pien</i> (see under <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Ts%27ao_Jung\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Ts&#39;ao Jung\">Ts'ao Jung</a></span>) and <i>Y\u00fceh-ya-t'ang ts'ung-shu</i> (see under <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Wu_Ch%27ung-y%C3%BCeh\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Wu Ch&#39;ung-y\u00fceh\">Wu Ch'ung-y\u00fceh</a></span>). Two works by him were included in the list of banned books of the eighteenth century, namely \u9d3b\u5bf6\u61c9\u672c <i>Hung-pao ying-p\u00ean</i> and <i>Ni W\u00ean-ch\u00eang i-shih</i> (\u907a\u8a69).\n</p>\n<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r15431518\">.mw-parser-output .wst-rule{background-color:currentcolor;color:inherit;width:auto;margin:2px auto 2px auto;height:1px;overflow:hidden}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.wst-rule-left{margin-left:0}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.wst-rule-right{margin-right:0}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.__uirule{background-color:#a2a9b1;color:inherit;border:0;margin-top:0.2em;margin-bottom:0.2em}</style><hr class=\"wst-rule wst-rule-left\" style=\"color:inherit;width:40%;\" />\n<p>[M.1/265/3a; M.3/252/3a; M.61/110/12b; <i>Ming-chi pei-l\u00fceh</i> (see bibl. <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Chang_Ch%27%C3%BCan\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Chang Ch&#39;\u00fcan\">Chang Ch'\u00fcan</a></span>) 21/2a; M.30/7/26a; <i>Shang-y\u00fc-hsien chih</i> (1898) 10/33a; <i>Ni W\u00ean-ch\u00eang kung nien-p'u</i>, with portrait, in the <i>Y\u00fceh-ya-t'ang ts'ung-shu</i>, vol. 215; a portrait by a contemporary painter, Ts\u00eang Ch'ing \u66fe\u9be8, is reproduced in the <i>Journal of Chekiang Provincial Library</i>, vol. III, No. 1.]\n</p>\n<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r15431519\">.mw-parser-output .wst-right{text-align:right}</style><div class=\"wst-right\" style=\"margin-right:2em;\">\n<p><span class=\"smallcaps\" style=\"font-variant:small-caps;\">George A. Kennedy</span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<p><br />\n&#32;\n</p>\n</div>\n</div>"},"links":[{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Chang Ch'\u00fcan"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/F\u00eang Ch'\u00fcan"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Huang Tao-chou"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Li Tz\u016d-ch'\u00eang"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Na-yen-ch'\u00eang"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Nien K\u00eang-yao"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Ts'ao Jung"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Wei Chung-hsien"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Wu Ch'ung-y\u00fceh"},{"ns":102,"exists":"","*":"Author:Arthur W. Hummel"},{"ns":102,"exists":"","*":"Author:George A. Kennedy"}],"sections":[],"wikitext":{"*":"{{Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period\n | volume   = 1\n | onlysection = ni yuan-lu\n | previous = Na-yen-ch'\u00eang\n | next     = Nien K\u00eang-yao\n | from     = 599\n | to       = 599\n | notes    =\n | section_author = George A. Kennedy\n}}{{DEFAULTSORT:Ni Y\u00fcan-lu}}"}}}